{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the city wakes, when the streets are blue and empty and everything feels briefly possible. The bakery on the corner breathes out warm air, the buses sigh at the curb, and somewhere a radio plays a song nobody is awake to hear. I walk without a destination, carrying yesterday loosely, letting my thoughts unspool like thread from a pocket. Some mornings I think about my grandmother's kitchen, where time moved slower and soup could fix almost any disappointment. She never measured anything, trusting her hands, and she hummed while she worked as if stirring could keep the world gentle. I try to remember her recipes, but memory is an unreliable narrator, changing spices, forgetting salt, inventing conversations that probably never happened. Maybe that is fine. Maybe stories are not meant to stay accurate; they are meant to stay alive, shifting each time we carry them into a new kitchen, a new city, a new loss. By seven, the spell thins. Doors open, phones glow, lists return, and everyone resumes pretending they know exactly where they are going. I join them, coffee in hand, but I keep a small private rebellion: I notice things. A child's red mitten on a fence, steam curling like handwriting, an old man feeding pigeons with ceremonial patience. These details are not useful, exactly, but they make the day porous, letting light through. And if I collect enough, perhaps life stops feeling ordinary and starts feeling quietly mine.",
